smoke: 0/6 surfaces verified; NOT VERIFIED: [filesystem: .code-forge/gate.yaml, filesystem: .code-forge/receipts, process: CLI exit codes/stderr, process: run_verify flags, config schema validation, python dependency: PyYAML]

--- Advisory ---
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:59-59 - read_required_cycles() now opens and parses .code-forge/gate.yaml on every verify. A missing/unreadable/malformed file or a non-positive/non-integer required_cycles silently falls back to DEFAULT_REQUIRED_CYCLES=3, so a repo that configured 1 but whose gate.yaml is temporarily unreadable or mid-write will get the stricter 3-cycle gate and fail unexpectedly. There is no lock around the file, so a concurrent editor/config writer can cause a partial read. Smoke: with gate.yaml containing verify.required_cycles: 1 and one clean cycle, verify must pass; after making gate.yaml unreadable, verify must fall back to 3 and fail with 'missing receipts: 3/9'.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:318-318 - run_verify now computes required = required_cycles * 3 and validates the last required_cycles consecutive cycles from .code-forge/receipts. Receipts are written by a separate code-forge review process; verify does not take a lock or snapshot. If the writer is still running or crashed after creating 1-2 receipts in a cycle, verify may see partial files and return missing/corrupt/not-consecutive failures. It also means a valid historical set can fail if the last N cycles are not consecutive. Smoke: with required_cycles: 1, write receipts c1p1-c1p3 and run verify (pass); write receipts for cycles 1 and 3 only and run verify (fail for non-consecutive cycles); run verify while a background review is writing receipts and confirm it either waits or fails cleanly instead of crashing.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/cli.py:1632-1632 - Invalid --required-cycles values are rejected in main after diff extraction has already happened and before run_verify; they print to stderr and return EXIT_CLI_ERROR. Scripts that rely on exit code 1 for any verify failure now see a different code for CLI misuse, and --quiet does not suppress this error output. Smoke: run 'code-forge verify --required-cycles 1' with one clean cycle and expect exit 0; run '--required-cycles 0' and expect CLI error code plus stderr; run '--quiet --required-cycles 0' and decide whether stderr should be empty.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:315-315 - run_verify is public and now takes required_cycles. At CLI call time its values are None (meaning read gate.yaml) or a positive int; argparse cannot produce bool and main rejects <1 before calling. Direct callers can pass anything: invalid values (0, -1, False, True, 1.0, '3') now make verify return FAIL before reading receipts. The existing hardened flag remains; the CLI leaves it at default True, and direct callers may pass True or False. Smoke: call run_verify directly with required_cycles=False on an empty receipt dir and assert it returns not passed with 'required_cycles' in the reason; call with None and assert it reaches the 'missing receipts' path.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/gate.schema.json:225-225 - gate.schema.json now declares verify.required_cycles as an integer with minimum 1. Any config loader that validates gate.yaml against this schema will accept verify sections that were previously invalid or rejected, and will reject values like 1.5 or '2' that read_required_cycles silently treats as default 3. This can make one command fail at config load while verify uses the default, so behavior depends on which loader runs. Smoke: run the full config loader with gate.yaml containing verify.required_cycles: 2 and confirm it loads; then try verify.required_cycles: 1.5 and verify the loader's behavior is consistent with read_required_cycles' fallback.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:61-61 - read_required_cycles imports yaml inside the try block. If PyYAML is absent in the deployed environment, ImportError is swallowed and required_cycles silently becomes 3, so a configured 1 is ignored. This is a silent dependency failure rather than a crash. Smoke: in an environment without PyYAML, set gate.yaml required_cycles: 1 and create one clean cycle; verify should pass if the config is honored, but it will fail because it used 3. If PyYAML is mandatory, import it at module load or declare the dependency so the failure is explicit.
